 develop actually related to the, the tariff issues. So we feel pretty confident that through the network of, you know, the multiple supply points, that we'll be able to, uh, overcome any particular issues coming from the virus. <crosstalk>. Yeah, I'm sorry, Bret, go ahead. I said, no, I was joking, but I had to get mt first virus question in there. Yeah. Okay. Yeah. All right, thanks a lot guys. Okay, thanks Bret. Thanks, Bret. Thank you. Our next question is coming from Rick Nelson of Stevens. Please go ahead. All right, thanks. Good morning. Like to- Good morning. follow up on whether, uh, last year, I think December, January, you also pointed to mild winter weather. Do you think this year, uh, was incrementally worse, uh, than than the prior year? I think if you look at, uh, you know, analyst reports that we've seen, uh, talking about, I guess the impact of the mild winter has had on not only our category, but I think other retail, I think the data would indicate that this has been more mild, uh, certainly versus last year. And I think we look at precipitation, uh, which was very important for us, the form of snow with our entire business. Certainly it's been, uh, a much, I think, more mild, uh, winter this year relative to last year. Okay, thanks. And Brett, any update on your partnership with Amazon, uh, what you're saying there on the install side and how you think your tire prices compare to theirs in the tire category over- overall. I'm curious about what happened with units in the period and the market share. Yep. So, uh, let's start with units, well, units were down two, uh, and average selling price was up, uh, one, uh, for the, for the, uh, for the quarter. Uh, how that compares, we don't have a lot, you know, of syndicated data. Of course, we can look to and give you definitive on that. We lean on, I guess our channel checks and information from our tire manufacturers that have good visibility across multiple brands and it feels like, you know, similar to other companies that have exposure to the North, in particular Northeast. But to the degree that we do, we feel like we are probably in line with what, you know, we picked up through suppliers and, and others. Um, as it relates to Amazon, I think we're still, you know, pleased with our relationship with them as well as other online tire retailers. Uh, we, uh, we will eventually expand on the relationship in FY21 to include all stores. We talked about last quarter, our priority has been, uh, what I mentioned on today's call, uh, completing the entire category management pricing tool, as well as the labor scheduling tool that are just for our organization, I think, a higher priority at this stage than expanding. Uh, but we are committed to expanding that relationship going forward. Uh, related to tire pricing, uh, as you know, Rick, the tire category is very complicated, you have, you know over 30,000 skews that, uh, that you need to manage effectively across multiple markets. And one of the reasons why we've invested in the tire category management tool is that, to do a couple of things. One is, give us much better visibility into demand dynamics defined by elasticity, uh, down to a skew level. And by having that visibility, it's gonna create a much more granular approach that we're gonna use for pricing. Uh, it'll allows us to price that, the skew level, uh, in our industry, uh, with the intent theme, strike that right balance between driving the right price to drive unit volume while preserving and expanding our entire margins through a right mix at right price. Uh, I'm encouraged we're in pilot form of that now, uh, we will be operational by Q1 of next year, uh, fully operational. Uh, the rollout of that it's pretty small in scale because we control our pricing centrally. So a small team of people will be trained up on using that system. So between now and Q1, we'll be testing in parallel to make certain we're confident with, uh, the results that we're seeing 