essive actions in the high pressure, uh, around course actions, based on what we see here with a lot of our data points and, uh, and the long duration of the downturn that we expect that business to have. Very helpful Vicente. Stay well. Thank you. You too Andy. Your next question comes from Julian Mitchell from Barclays. Your line is open. Hi, good morning. Um- Good morning Julian. M- morning. Maybe just a, a first question on that point on decremental margins. Um, so if you could help us understand perhaps the phasing of the, uh, cost synergies through the year and also of that, um, 40 to 50 million of, of other costs out actions, and should those mean that decremental margins narrow, or not necessarily depending… on mix and some other things? He, maybe break it down into the two buckets, uh, as, as you suggest to me on the 40 to 50 that we spoke about, uh, that are kind of more related to, uh, discretionary or kind of volume related. Those are largely second quarter and the third quarter, uh, with a good majority, I would say more, more so on the, uh, on the second quarter. Uh, from my, from the cost synergy perspective, million dollars, uh, you know, <inaudible> probably 80 to $90 million of end year. Um, you know, growth and downturn I would say that it's kind of consistent third quarter and fourth quarter, while the other, roughly, you know, 10 to $20 million that comes from procurement, it is really more weighted towards that kind of Q3 and Q4. Then maybe just my, uh, second question, um, for you or for Emily around the free cash flow, is that a good performance in Q1? Uh, just wondered, you know, you- the assumptions around a, a break even free cash. But assume we play out, um, you know, what kind of, you know, of sales are down called it 20, 25% for the year? What type of free cash flow conversion, uh, should we expect? You know, how do you see working capital moving? You heard, I think, in the free cash that $63 million, uh, of transaction and separation cash cost in Q1, what's the rough, uh, assumptions for the year? Um, yes. We're very pleased with, with the Q1 cash performance, as you, as you saw there Julian. And we've been managing cash, um, you know, from… to put in good processes and strong controls around it, uh, given the current crisis. We, we, we expect cash to still be… to move forward, but certainly a longer cash cycle, as we move through, um, wi- And we're really managing, you know, payments as to the collections were receiving, to maintain our strong cash flow and liquidity positions. Um, you know, what the future holds? Um, a lot of that's, you know, gonna depend o- um, for sure, but we know we'll maintain our cash position in our liqui- And how about the cost? Is there any very rough g- I'd post for the year, uh, in light of about 63 million in Q1? There'll be some, some incremental… Um, I don't have the, I don't have the figure at my fingertips at the moment Julian, but I can, I can get back to you on that. Your next question comes from Michael Holleran, from Viad. Good morning everyone? Hope everyone's doing well. So, so could we just talk about the, the synergy funnel you referenced? You know, what, what are some of the incremental sources o- You originally identified 250… M- Maybe talk about the difference you've seen, no longer terms some of the revenue synergy opportunities you see. Yeah, a, a, a as you recall, you know, we always said that, uh, that we were going for, uh, you know, as we with 60 days pays into, uh, into, into, into the transaction, we have all these serviceability as to what that funnel could, uh, could, could potentially be. Roughly the a $100 million comes from a combination of structural savings. Uh, a rationalization, kind of, non-manufacturing, alluded to on the investor side. We have now a pretty good database o- all the, uh, uh, the locations across the world. And that is given us a very good way for us to really understand and rationalize, uh, not so much the m- are co