hares… Okay our next question is from Alejandro Chavela with Credit Suisse. Hello guys. Thanks for taking my question and uh, stay safe. Uh, just on competitive dynamics in Columbia, I know you already mentioned a little bit, but if you could comment a little bit with regards to the, to the improvement that you are seeing in import parity pricing and the sliced market share that you had in the quarter. Uh, could you comment a little bit more on what you expect coming forward in terms of market share, or what are you seeing from, from the market as a whole, with, with the pricing increase that you made in the first quarter? Thank you very much. Thank you, Alejandro. Uh, the market in Columbia was behaving in a, in a very good way until the, uh, first half of, of March. Uh, with the, um, increase in demand of last year close to 500,000 tons plus the 7% increase in demand through February. The reality is that the competitive landscape was, in my opinion, a positive… Um, nobody was really competing on price because of the huge gap in pricing that we have vis-a-vis import parity prices and the intention of, uh, uh, of uh, everybody to reward the cost of capital of the assets that we have in, in Colombia. Um, I would expect that scenario to, um, continue the same way going forward once the market start to, to recover. So we are extremely pleased with the, the rising of the market in April and in May, and in our opinion our competitive position has strength, uh, in this restart of the, of the economy. Because of the, those relationships that align with our clients on the technical, um, assistance that we provide to them, in our opinion, have a superior value proposition for the industrial segment of the market, and we're, uh, in my opinion the only cement player in Colombia who access directly a wider range of hybrid storage in the countries. We are going <unk> for more than 5,000 hybrid storage. So in our opinion, our value proposition will be an important asset for us going forward. Uh, we don't foreseen any valid continued prices no matter what scenario of the month plays out going forward. This was to be an significant gap in prices vis-a-vis import parity prices. Juan, I wanted to add that, uh, during this quarter, we gained market share with the investor business where we suffered, uh, the most by the end of, of last year. Uh, particularly in the ready-mix business. So, uh, we are, we are, uh, as strong as, as we've always been in the industrial part of the, of the market. Uh, the loss of market share has been, uh, mostly focused on, on the retail business, that is where we have seen, uh, you know, recovering prices faster, and we have gone, uh, as usual, faster than our competitors in, in that market. Um, and, uh, and I, and I think, you know, it's pretty much a, a very good move to have done it before this crisis, uh, because otherwise, you know, our competitors will be, uh, as Juan was saying, uh forced or, or, um, incentivized, you know, to, to, to recover, uh, value as well for their, for, for their companies in a, in a, you know, more stressful, uh, market. So we are, we are trying, you know, to, to recover the markets share, uh, with, uh, healthier prices, you know, as we are seeing today and, and, um, you know, import parity prices have been, uh, uh, a very good indicator of, of, uh, of, you know, the, the direction where, where national prices should go and in this case, uh, this is going in the, you know, in, in, in the, in the right direction. Understood, thank you very much. Very clear. Thank you. Our next question is from Stephanie <unk> with CrediCorp Capital. Good morning. Thank you very much for the presentation. I have two questions. Um, my first question is regarding the distribution of your taskforce. Uh, what proportion would you say is fixed, and what proportion would you say is variable? And then my second question is, uh, regarding the competitive dynamic in Honduras. You mentioned in the presentation tha