cause other, other countries such as, uh, Panama and Dominican Republic, we have a very competitive, um, construction negotiated with local energy suppliers. Thank you, Juan. Thank you, Mike. Thank you, Camilo. Juan, just a compliment on what you said for the US standpoint, with, with respect to diesel. I mean, we have seen some movements, um, at the competition in our ready mix business. But again, I think you're correct, we feel like we're gonna see more of that as the year goes on, because of what's happening with the, the whole situation in general. Thank you, Bill. Thank you for that. Can I just then follow up, if all those savings, uh, from all those fuel savings you've talked about, are they in the reset numbers? Or are they on top of the reset numbers? They will be on top of the reset, uh, numbers. Okay. Uh, this was also variable cost and we are basically, um, addressing fixed costs and topics. Excellent. Thank you very much and stay safe. Thank you. And our last question is Juan <inaudible> with <unk>. Please go ahead. Hi, good morning. Thank you. And could you provide, uh, could you provide us with an overview for the working capital requirements of the business? We saw an increase in, in inventories and, and receivables. So, can you tell us how these, these accounts are, are behaving? Thank you. Thank you, Juan. Thank you for your question. Carlos, uh, can you, uh, give us some clue about the working capital management? Hi, Juan. The working capital… The behavior of the working capital so far from the beginning of the cycle has been very good. We are controlling almost a big level of of the organization, the excom, we are controlling almost every, every one of the <unk> of the, of the purchase orders. I think that that's necessary in a situation like this one, um, but jus- jus- just to give you a little bit more color: in the case of the Colombia, like Tomas has said very well, uh, we had stopped the operations from the, from the last 10 days of March. T