 Basically, the, the program that we have had with the, the, the, uh, the, uh, our personnel is more because the, the lock-downs, they leave their house and the lack of, of, of, of, of care they have with, with connection with some family members. And, and, and most of the people that have had, uh, the, the pandemic goes to the, uh, house, not ne- not really from, from, from the traffic to the, to the work or the store. So, we are optimistic. But the thing is that, you know, we are feeling now the pain that the economic downturn is becoming, is, is, is, is staying here. And, and, and, and we'll, we, we, we are adapting the, the, the offering, too, but we are cautiously optimistic. Yeah, that's what we have to be. Well, thank you very much. Thanks. Our next question will come from Bob Ford of Bank of America. Hey, thank you. Good morning, everybody. And, and thank you for, for taking my question. Eduardo, on your opening comments, you mentioned the 24 closures that are announced. I was curious. Is it really for a wider assessment of the store base? And, and, and how do you think about the footprint right now in terms of maybe reallocating those resources to other locations or, or maybe just, uh, trimming it in anticipation of, uh, you know, more traditional headwinds in terms of the, the slowdown in the economy? Well, as I said, the, a lot of the lock-downs that we had ha- had the restrictions of alcoholic, uh, curfews, or, or lock-downs or the possibility of open stores in certain, uh, commercial areas. And, and, uh, that was the number that we had. Around 50% have these kind of restrictions. As I said now, uh, out of the 19,000, uh, stores, there are around 7,000 that might, that have had some kind of restrictions. For instance, in the state of Tamaulipas, the previous weekend, we were not allowed, uh, to, to, to sell. We ha- we have to, we have to clo- close, not only not sell alcohol, but to close the, the, uh, completely the store. And again, I think the local authorities are trying to best, their best to adapt and, and, and, and, and have countermeasures against this pandemic. Some are effective, and some are not, uh, I would say. And, and, and, but the thing is, we try to, to lobby and, and understand what the authorities is planning to do and help them to accomplish the, the objectives, not necessarily by, by, by, um, unstabilizing the, the, the way we, we, we work with the stores. Um, uh, I think another problem is, really, through the lockdown, the, the lack of social mobility and the social distancing. Night shifts are very, very weak. Um, and, and we have had some, uh, besides the, the lockdown restrictions that we have with some authorities or selling of alcohol in some, uh, some stores. Also, we are really closing some of the night shifts because, again, there is no, no, no consumers around. So, but again, we think that it will be temporary, and, uh, and we are adapting the best we can. Thank you very much. Thank you, Bob. And again, as a remind, that is star one to ask a question. And if you find that your question has been answered, you can remove yourself from the queue by pressing star two. We will now go to Miguel Tortolero of GBM. Hi. Good morning, everyone. Thanks for the, thanks for the questions. Uh, considering the timing of this crisis that, that came right after you bought or invested in a couple of companies, which of them would you say that have had the most challenging start now in the, in the sense of direction? Uh, you know, we, we're very happy with the, uh, with our partners and with the companies we acquired. Uh, they are in the Jan-San business. And as, as you imagine, Jan has had a, uh, uh, uh, uh, a great, uh, uh, demand, uh, for, for the products. Out of the two, uh, WAXIE is more focused to Jan-San and North American had a more diverse platform. And in fact, North American had, had to sell, uh, to some, uh, uh, hospitality, uh, uh, uh, customers. And I think probably that is the one that has suffered the, the most. But I think both companies have adapted tremendously to, in this environment. They've been able to, to protect their, their, their employees and prot- and, and, and, and, but the, at the very same time, trying to serve their customers. So, we're extraordinarily happy with them. We have great partners. And I think that, uh, we are, uh, going to build an extraordinary platform with the help of them, and, and, uh, we look forward with a very optimistic, uh, uh, uh, uh, future. Yeah, I, I think I would add, um, uh, Miguel, this is Juan. Um, even on the, on the Jetro Restaurant Depot, which is also one of the recent investments, uh, which being as exposed as they are to the restaurant sector, uh, one would think or at least one would have thought a few months ago, uh, that this would be a complicated period for them. And I'm not saying it, it hasn't been complicated, but certainly, uh, they have been, uh, adjusting and navigating the situation, uh, quite frankly, exceeding, exceeding at least my personal expectations, uh, which, uh, kind of confirms our view that this is a very well-positioned and very well-run company. So, they have been doing, uh, as I said, better than, better than expected adjusting. And I think their customers as well, uh, the little restaurants, adapting to, you know, curbside delivery. Um, and things begin to open up in many of the markets that they serve. So, overall, I would say, on the restaurant depot front, um, you know, a positive surprise. Great. Thank you both. <crosstalk> And now, we will take a question from Marcella Recchia of Credit Suisse. And Marcella, if you could check your mute function. We're unable to hear you. Hi. Uh, are you listening me now? Yes. Please go ahead with your question. Yes. Okay. Hi, Juan. Hi, Eduardo. I hope you are well. Uh, just two quick que- que- questions here from my side. The first one is about G&A. Uh, I saw that, uh, basically, your G&A, uh, increased, uh, across the business 