h, you, your, your numbers are actually very good. Uh, there was a very little contraction there. You mentioned that the beer shortage obviously affects your profitability, but that was offset by, by the service category. On the recovery, uh, do you think that you could actually see an expansion in your gross margin as the, I don't know if the service category, uh, really remains strong, uh, and the beer clearly could recover. Uh, could you please elaborate a bit on, on the trend for, for the gross, gross margin for, for OXXO? Well, let, let me give you a summa- our business, our suppliers and us are concerned with the lack, the, uh, lack of consumer, uh, uh, uh, uh, economic strength, but what we're trying to do is, is to come up with, with different offerings to, to really estimate dema- the demand. Um, and here, we are, uh, we have our, our, our, our, our suppliers and our partners and, and trying to do something, uh, great for them, for the consumer in, in, in, uh, in, in, in COVID times. But I don't know, uh, Juan. Why do you go and, and go for the, uh, details of the numbers, please? Sure. Uh, I, I think, on the gross margin, I mean, historically, or at least for, for a, a long time now, the two main drivers of gross margin expansion have been the financial services category and, uh, you know, uh, the commercial income that we, that we receive from our, from our, uh, supplier partners. Right now, as Eduardo ha- has said, um, you know, I'm, I think a positive surprise has been the resilience and even the, you know, uh, faster growth of financial services during the downturn, um, as consumers have kind of made sure that they're keeping current with their, with their memberships and their utilities, and, and, um, you know, realizing or, or taking advantage of the fact that it's, it's a lot easier to go to OXXO than, than to go to the bank, and it's less time-consuming. And so, even though financial services had been contracting or at least it has been growing less in recent quarters, in the last two, three quarters, uh, this quarter, it actually, uh, the growth came, came in the, in the high single digits, which was a, a positive surprise, and I think contributes to the, to a high gross margin. The other question, uh, on commercial income is a little bit trickier, uh, because, you know, so, uh, some of those agreements, as Eduardo also referred to, um, are, are tied to, to volume. And so, it's hard to predict. I mean, we, we do need, uh, volumes to come up, uh, transactions to go up, for commercial income to go up, uh, you know, in consequence. So, I would say visibility for gross margin is, is, is not as clear as, as we would like. Um, I wouldn't necessarily predict at this point that we will continue to, to expand it, um, you know, more than, more than trend. Uh, but at least, during the, during the second quarter, uh, it did, uh, kind of hold its own. Thank you. I think it's very clear. Thank you. Thank you. Uh, I would also add that, probably, the, uh, uh, there, there were a small increase also in, in, in, in, uh, the lack of beer also, uh, shift demand to, uh, to some other alcoholic beverages. And, um, that, those, uh, have, uh, uh, that compensated the margin a little bit, too. And that, it actually brings up an interesting point, uh, Eduardo, because, you know, obviously, you're, you're, you're right. Uh, consumers did, uh, shift to, uh, higher consumption of spirits, uh, during the quarter. Um, and interestingly, I think what, what the OXXO, uh, our OXXO colleagues were observing in recent weeks was that, even as the beer came back, that the volume for spirits was still, uh, holding up strong. So, maybe, you know, if, uh, in the, in the effort to look for the, for the silver lining or the glass half full, uh, you know, as consumers realize what the assortment and the, the pricing is for, for spirits, uh, at the OXXO stores, that this becomes a category that, that is, uh, you know, that is stronger than, that historically because, historically, we haven't sold as much spirits as, as we are selling right now. So, hopefully, this remains after the, after the pandemic is, is, uh, gone. And we'll move on to our next question, and that will be from Alan Alanis of Santander. Thank you so much. Good morning, everyone. Eduardo… Hello, Alan. Hello? Did, did we lose Alan? It looks like we may have lost Alan. We'll go next to Ulises Argote. Hi, everyone. Thanks for the, for the space for questions. So, here, uh, I wanted to, to get your thoughts a little bit on, on any potential, uh, product portfolio rationalization there, there in OXXO. Uh, and maybe if you can share any, any color on what you're seeing in terms of, of the shift in, in mix more recently now in July, maybe going back a little bit more to the, to the more profitable categories that you guys have there. Uh, thank you. You know, unless we think that this, uh, lack of mobility will stay for a very long period of time, um, uh, which we not, which we, we don't think it is the case, I think we, we be- we have to adapt better, and we have to adapt more to the, um, to the, um, um, lack of, of, of consumer economic, uh, power. And, and by, by probably with a different assortment, probably the, the, the, looking for, for cheaper or, or less expensive products, um, our returnable, uh, uh, cun- uh, uh, uh, soft drinks, uh, is, is not as strong as we, uh, uh, we should have it now. And, and, and those kind of shift, we can, we can foresee it for the future. But not necessarily in, in the types of, of, of, of, of consumer demand, of, of, of, of, uh, thirst or, or, or, uh, breakfast, or, or people gathering, depending on, uh, we think that, eventually, uh, those, those, uh, type of, of consumer demand will stay. It is more the lack of economic power, the consumer that we're more concerned than, than the, uh, real shift of, of our consumer occasions. That, that's perfect. And, and a bit more on the, on the short term kind of thinking now on, on June-July, after the, the beer category's backend, etc. Uh, do you have any, any color that you can share with us kind of there on, on the shift back, let's say, in, in mix? Yeah. Juan? Yeah. I, I, I think, you know, one, one thing to kind of highlight, and I think Eduardo touched on it in his, uh, opening remarks, was that, um, the quarter itself was a little bit of a, uh, I don't know, if a V shape is a little bit of a strong, uh, descriptor. But certainly, the, the, the trough of the, of the, of the trend was, was the month of May. Um, and we believe that, uh, we hope and believe that will mark the, the lowest point of this downturn, uh, should be the month of May. Um, and then, as, as beer comes back, uh, it, it, it helps everything, right? It, it helps operating leverage, and it helps margins, and, uh, generally begins to, to turn this into a, uh, uh, a more traditional downturn, so, so to speak. Um, and so, you know, the, uh, as Eduardo said in the beginning, we, we are seeing improving, improving metrics and improving, uh, traffic. And, and, uh, really, the, the one concern I have is not to convey the, the view that, you know, once the, the mobility restrictions are, are gone, that we go back to normal, uh, immediately, right? Because we are seeing these, uh, symptoms and, and signs of a, of a more traditional economic-driven, uh, downturn. But, uh, again, you know, you, when you go, when you go dry, and this has never happened before, on the beer side, uh, obviously, you have both of our suppliers on the beer front, uh, working hard to restock the, the shelves. Um, consumers themselves are, are probably buying a little bit more beer than they normally do, uh, to make sure they don't go dry again. And so, you know, you, you're seeing, uh, positive trends on, on, on some of these, uh, important categories, especially beer. Um, so, I would expect that to, to continue. And, uh, you know, we, we will obviously make the adjustments. Uh, Eduardo referenced some in terms of, of, uh, packaging and, and, and mix for some important categories. Um, I think we need to, to be careful and work hard at, at understanding how, you know, how quickly and, and what the new normal eventually ends up looking like, uh, in the sense that we will, I'm sure, adapt our value proposition to that, uh, whether it means, uh, you know, some incremental SKUs or a shift in the mix, um, and that sort of thing. So, so, we're, we're still not, uh, at the end of this, uh, but we know more about how the consumer is, uh, adjusting habits than we did a couple months ago, um, and we are acting in, in consequence. <unk> Yeah. <unk> And we will now go back to Alan Alanis of Santander. <laugh> Let's see if this time works. Thank you so much. Thanks for taking my question. And again, good morning. Hope you guys are, and your families are safe. Um, Eduardo or Juan, um, I guess, I mean, we, on the sell side, most of us have a buy rating on the stock, I mean, because of the, of, of the luxury valuation of OXXO implied here. Now, the, the, the result of this quarter, I guess, the question is, uh, is, is this result putting to question the business model of OXXO in the long run? This is the first quarter ever that you closed stores. Uh, so, I guess, the specific question is, um, and, and sorry if I missed it. Uh, what, what's the expectation regarding store openings going forward? And the second thing, and this is a question we get a lot, and I'm, I'm sure Juan also gets it a lot, and Eduardo, it is, why the reluctance to stay away from e-commerce, um, and invest here in job? You've been saying every time you guys mentioned the copilot with Amazon or so forth, the stock reacts positively. Why not invest in a, in a basement or in a second floor in as, in as many offices as possible to use them as distribution for e-commerce and, and leverage that, especially given the, the changes of the pandemic will cost? And the third and last question has to do with, with c