e Ja- Ja- uh, Jan-San business, we are, um, um, uh, I think we made, uh, uh, the right decision with the companies we're with it, with the partners that we have, and the platforms that have been established and the opportunities, too, for integration, uh, uh, and, and, going forward, for a na- more national, uh, uh, uh, uh, footprint. Um, as, as I said, we don't plan to open up a new vector for growth. I think the ones are there, and we just have to be very keen. And, and probably those ne- short-term, uh, uh, uh, pandemic disturbance, uh, uh, cope with it, align better, uh, make efficiencies and, and probably make some decisions to adjust ourselves to this new environment. But we have great platforms for growth, uh, in, uh, in the future. Juan? Yeah. No. I, I think I would, uh, just following up on what you just said a while ago, um, you know, on the whole Jan-San, uh, platform, uh, there's certainly, you know, this is an industry that is very highly fragmented, um, where you don't really have a huge incumbent, um, as a platform that we are forming by combining, uh, WAXIE in North American, already becomes, uh, one of the larger players, um, with, uh, you know, the, the, the, WAXIE, since it has, uh, uh, kind of a bias to the West Coast of the US, uh, North American, out of Chicago has more of a Midwest, uh, all the way down to Florida coverage. But you really don't have, uh, players with anything resembling a big national presence. And so, the synergy component, um, the, uh, the aspiration to integrate more of these assets, um, at the right time and in the right way, it's a big part of, I think, the, the aspiration that, that we have for that business. Scale is a beautiful thing. We, we, we have, uh, been the beneficiaries of, of, of scale in, in many of our, uh, businesses. Um, and this is another one where we think that could work, uh, its, its magic if done at, in the right way. Um, I think you also, uh, mentioned at the end of your question something about an 