k the, you know, the challenges from the, from the relabeling involve more, you know, the costs and the, kind of the, the operating, uh, conundrums of, you know, changing labels or adjusting your packaging, that sort of thing, but not so much in terms of the incremental information that the consumer will receive. There are, there are other categories where, uh, maybe the, the consumer has not been as, as, as exposed to nutritional information. Um, and I think, for those, there, there, there will be a period of adaptation. Uh, but to Eduardo's point, uh, you know, I think also is, is, is, it's very well positioned, and the, the, the flexibility and the, the, the level of, uh, dialogue and cooperation with suppliers, uh, leads, I think, to, to what should be a relatively smooth transition when this eventually becomes a, uh, uh, a fact. Uh, during this transition, uh, Juan and Eduardo, uh, do you foresee, uh, um, the risk of a, uh, a more aggressive markdown, just to get rid of the inventory that is with the old labeling and befo- or, or suppliers are going to, uh, assume the cost, um, you know, of, of that operation? I think that could impact your, your 4Q results or perhaps part of your 3Q results. No, but I, I, I think if we, we, uh, I ne- but I never, uh, the turnover of inventory in, in, in, in OXXO goes very fast. And if there will be some opportunities there, we will, then we'll continue to make some more money. Uh, uh, yes, I, I, I think it'll be, yeah, probably, uh, uh, an alternative. I don't know if Juan… No, I, I agree with you that, uh, our inventory levels and, uh, you know, tend to be small and, and there, the turnover is just as fast. And I'm sure we will work with suppliers and come up, come up with promotional activity as required. Um, and I don't think it's something that is, that is causing us to lose a lot of sleep at this point. Thank you very much for the clarification. Thank you. Thanks, Gustavo. Thank you. And ladies and gentlemen, that is all 