ns. I mean, we are basically focusing all our expenses in IT to support the backbone and to be ready for what's coming. Um, so in that sense, that's one of your priority. Secondly, we are taking a look, uh, uh, and we're doing a deep dive in terms of, uh, all our infrastructure and, um, we're not gonna get, take, I mean, um, wrong decisions in terms of closing down a big number of, uh, of, um, branches or anything like that. I think, we're going to be rationally looking at each of the micro-markets that we have divided the country into, and we will decide, um, with evolution of the economy, what should happen there. So, uh, but we're going to be very strict. If you have seen the growth of the past, uh, four or five years in terms of branches, we actually have decreased the number of total branches. We have just, uh, we have been very intelligent in the way we have managed that. What we have done is upgraded, uh, the current and the most important branches, which we are going to basically be, uh, taking a little bit more time than the time that we, uh, were thinking of, of, um, upgrading all the branches that are not up to the standard. So, this is actually what we're doing. We're going to basically rationalize all, uh, all, uh, all expending and do it on where the most important thing is, that is basically the IT platform and the digitalization of the bank. So, in that sense we're going to be very concentrated on that. Uh, we also believe that, I mean, given that we have been able to operate the bank under these circumstances, we have a huge amount of people basically working from home, uh, you basically get to understand just actually how really, how much people do you need? Uh, what can you rationalize, et cetera? So, what you're going to see from us is that we're going to be very, uh, responsible in the way we manage that and, uh, concentrate on those points. We are working currently on that plan. We are working on our three-year plan, and, uh, and, uh, we're basically including all these, uh, inward solution. Maybe, uh, as a follow up. mm-hmm <affirmative>. Just some comments on what you'd seen, uh, throughout your peers and the Santander group in, in the region or elsewhere, are there other things you think that could be applied to Mexico that could help, um, perhaps on the side of the banking system? Perhaps, in terms operationally or IT, or, or any factors at all? Thank you. Wh- what we're doing basically is, for example, I mean in terms of developing, uh, these, the platforms, et cetera, but we're doing it, we're doing it, uh, now instead of each country basically developing the platforms, we are doing it, uh, together. So, that basically is going to help out quite a lot. So, uh, it's not Mexico, for example, right now in Mexico we're developing the new super app and the new super app is going to be used in the, in, in, in, in all the other countries, you know. For example, the onboarding, uh, for SMEs, we are using the one that is being developed at the, in Europe. So, all those type, type of things are going to help us out basically in reducing our cost, uh, for the, the infrastructure that we're developing and we're working together much more within the group in order to do that. So, you're going to see that, I mean, in the cost of, uh, basically developing things and it's going to come down due to the fact that we're doing it together with the rest of our, uh, um, sister banks, so… So, so those t- Great. Thank you very much. <crosstalk> implementing at this ti- at this point. Thank you. I appreciate it. You're welcome too. The next question comes from the line of Jorge Kuri from Morgan Stanley. Please go ahead. Hi, good morning everyone. Um, I, I wondered what's about net interest margin. Um, you, you, you pointed out that you expect further, um, complications in the next couple of quarters given, uh, falling grades and weak, uh, loan demands, and, um, mixed deterioration. Um, as, as you look at 2021, uh, in the context of what you said on n- not a strong economic recovery next year, and with average rates next year being m- m- much lower than 2020, if, if indeed rates end up at 4%. And w- what is the expectation for the net interest margin next year, and for NII? I- i- is it possible that we're likely to see another year of, um, NII compression? And, and, to, to what extent that can be offset, uh, elsewhere in the PML? And, and my second question is on, um, the, on the coverage ratio. And I, and I know that banks, you, you know, the, the banks normally don't target a coverage ratio, I get it. Um, but, but evidently the, the, the size of your reserves, uh, have a relationship to the size of your logbook, the size of your MPLs, and the size of the amount of loans that are on their restructuring. Um, and so when I look at your CapEX ratio at 100, 138%, it looks low relative to where Banorte ended up at 201%, and <unk> 185%. So I, I wonder, you know, why, why, w- w- why not just, you know, take a much bigger charge on provisions this quarter? Um, you know, evidently there is, there's, there's a lot of uncertainty ahead and, um, the more the better, a- and, and so is this piecemeal just going to, you know, potentially put more pressure on your earnings going forward rather just, you know, take a really big one swoop and, you know, r- raise your coverage to north of 200%. Um, and, and is that likely maybe something you will do in the third quarter? Thank you. Um, look, Jorge. I will ask, um, this is Hector. I will ask, um, I will answer the first part, and then I will ask Didier to complete on the, on the basic numbers of NII, okay? Um, I mean, first of all, I mean, if, if you saw the presentation, we are basically changing the mix of, um, of the credit loan portfolio, okay? First of all, I mean, um, if you have seen, I mean, uh, we have decreased quite a lot our exposure, unfortunately, to SMEs given the lack of, uh, warranties from the government, from Nafinsa, okay? So, uh, the portfolio has dropped almost 10%. So, in that sense, uh, that basically is a high-yield portfolio, which, um, is going to continue to shrink unless we, we see some warranties from the government. And you, if you see our portfolio, which is quite important, we have like 62% of it already warranted by Nafinsa, okay? The corresponding volume. This basically goes to answer your second part of the question in terms of, uh, the amount of, uh, the amount of reserves that we have done. Uh, then you see that, uh, also, we see that, for example, consumer credit and, uh, consumer loans are going to decrease as well. So, the mix is completely changing. That we're going, the… I mean, we're going basically back to where we were, wh- where we were five years ago when we had much more exposure to corporates and to medium-sized companies than to consumer loans. Remember that we have that different mix, okay? So, that's why NII is shrinking. On the other side, what we've also been doing, it was with pricing, and we are very s- strict on pricing risk, okay? So, um, we've been increasing the margin on the corporate loans quite significantly due to that fact, you know, that we are basically looking at the, the risk that we are taking and, uh, we are looking very closely at the ratings of those companies, and we are pricing risk accordingly, okay? This is not a situation in which we want to spend the port- credit portfolio just to expand it. We are basically doing it just to the companies that we believe that have a future, and that will be there in the next few years, and both we're pricing the risk accordingly. So, in that sense, you're going to see us going towards that. Also, the other concentration is mortgages, which we, uh, which we believe, I mean, there is a sector still, uh, of growth. I mean, as you know, there is a lack of a, or, or a big deficit in terms of homes in Mexico. So, we believe there is still a part of the population that can afford a mortgage. Uh, mortgages penetration in Mexico was quite low versus the rest and also loan penetration is quite low. So, we believe there is a chance to, to, to continue to penetrate that sector. And maybe on payrolls, on some situations, we will basically be, uh, also lending in that, in the, in that part. I mean, and auto loans also, we have seen some interesting, uh, development there with very cautious approach to it. So, in that sense, the mix of the portfolio is going to change and probably Didier can let you know exactly how do we see NII evolving. In terms of what you were asking in terms of, um, the reserves, uh, we, maybe, we've actually been doing reserves accordingly wh- to what we believe we are doing, uh, in terms of, uh, uh, how the portfolio is, uh, is, uh, behaving at this point. What I can tell you is that the portfolio not on payments holidays is behaving much, much better than we believed that it was going to t- that it was going to turn. Actually, I'm quite surprised about it. Hopefully, uh, it continues that way. But in that sense, we are basically being- in my opinion, uh, being very responsible in the amount of, uh, of reserves that we are making at this point. Also, we have done a tremendous job, uh, with the data, uh, hopes that we have in reviewing, um, the portfolio on holidays. And that portfolio is being divided, as we said, in 36 clusters, okay? These clusters have been divided upon ri- upon the amount of risk that we have on each particular situation. And in some of these portfolios, uh, even before the payment holiday ends, we are already talking to clients and discussing the way, uh, uh, that, that we could get paid on those particular situations because what we believe is that whoever gets paid first is the one that, is the one that is going to get paid. So, we are basically being very aggressive on that sense to talk to our clients and basically trying to get that and to recover the portfolio. As you have seen, 55% of our branches have been turned into, uh, collecting branches. I mean, uh, to basically this, uh, uh, we're dedicating our executives to collect. We have divided them up in the sectors that we believe are the ones that are, the ones that are going to be much more important in that, or that are going to be hit the most in the economy. So, in that sense, I believe we're very well prepared. Although, I mean, or nevertheless, uh, is always, I mean, a lot of uncertainty in what's happening, and we didn't want to go overboard and we believe it's the right, uh, the right approach is the one that we have made. But we will continue to see over the next few months evolution. What I can tell you is that we're going to be very conservative in the way we manage that, okay? Um, Didier, I don't know if you want to complete. Absolutely, eh, absolutely, Hector. I would like to, to complement on what you mentioned. Eh, eh, great talking to you, Jorge. Long time, no speak. Um, eh, in terms of, uh, eh, NIMs, you know, eh, definitely, you know, as we laid out in the presentation, we see significant, uh, pressures. And let me just give you some background information that I think it's, it's, eh, it's quite relevant. Um, the, the speed at which interest rates have come down in Mexico is, is, is, is significant. You know, just in this quarter, eh, the average of the reference, uh, reference rate came down 123 basis points. That's the, the, the fastest decline that, eh, we've seen, eh, in the recent history. We, we didn't saw a, a, an increase of those, eh, you know, of that magnitude when, eh, when interest rates were, were coming up, okay. So that's one thing to have in mind, the, the, the impact that you see in this quarter, I think is, is probably most <inaudible> that we're going to see in a relative basis, relative to the first quarter of this year and relative to the second quarter of last year, okay. Um, also, bear in mind that when interest rates were close to 4% as we probably agree with you that that's where we're headed, you know, are close to 5%, you know, and this was, the second and third quarter of 2016. Uh, I think that, eh, the, the number that, that we reported, the 448% in this quarter, you know have to do with, with what I was referring to, you know, the speed at which interest rates, eh, eh came down, and also on top of that, you have the, the, the change in mix on our loan side and also, the fact that in order to protect the, eh, the income from eh, interest rates coming down, we, we increased our pay, interest securities portfolio significantly, you know, and those are, you know, lower margin, uh, eh, assets relative to the, the the loan portfolio. Just to, you know, share with you, uh, we've bought, in this account, you know, both divestment and maturities. The overall growth in our pay investment and securities portfolio is 96 billion over the last 12 months. So, um, what we expect coming down the road, you know, pay for, the rest of 2020 and, eh, for next year, I think that, uh, you know, having said that, there's still a lot of uncertainty, you know. I think that probably the, the most severe numbers in terms of name are the ones that we saw this quarter, you know, there's probably, you know, 10 to 15 basis points, eh, probably further adjustment or slight, eh, improvement for the rest of the year. Okay? And next year, I think that, eh, we'll, we'll probably get to see names similar or slightly above to the ones that we were seeing, eh, eh, in this quarter. Now, in regarding to the current ratio, you're totally right in the sense that we don't target <inaudible>. That, that's, uh, resulting number. And, also, you know, when you, eh, make reference to, you know, our number 138% looks low relative to <inaudible>, the first quarter this year, they had 140%, uh, in this, in the second quarter, they did, as we did also, <inaudible> increase the provisions for different to us, they made a significant charge off, 4.6 billion pesos. So that's why, you know, they're, their increased to 200%. Uh, I'm probably just elaborating, elaborating, eh, the more on what information, you know, uh, the letter of provisions that we have, are in line with the expected losses that we, we estimate. Um, you know, you might have a, or you might question whether we, you know, this is enough or not, you know and let me share with you that, you know, under current circumstances, we, we have definitely done different types of sensitivity analysis or scenario analysis, okay? So, if <inaudible> recovery in the Mexican economy, you know, we have, with these quarters, eh, provisions, we are, you know, more than covered, you know, for the rest of the year. Now, do we think that there's going to be a type recovery in the Mexican economy? I don't think so. You know, it's very, you know low probability, you know. Now, we have a <inaudible>, you know, the, the our leave of provisions, according to other types of <inaudible> more a U type or an adverse scenario having agility contraction of 12.5%, you know, and under those circumstances, these quarters provisions are not enough for the entire year. There could be close to 60 to 75%, depending on the magnitude of the <inaudible> eh, contraction that that you assume, okay? Eh, eh, so, so there should be, uh, direct correlation between expected losses and provisions and the provisions that we are putting forward, we think that with information that we have, uh, are quite strong to cover, you know, expected losses if the, the, the economy ends up contracting more than we are expecting right now, definitely we will need to provide a provision of provisions. Something that I, I encourage you to look at if you haven't done so, eh, yet, you know, the, the <inaudible> bank <inaudible>, um, they released in the stability, financial stability report, published a few weeks ago, an analysis regarding NPL for the entire system. And they, what they did is look at information from the entire banking system from the last 20 years and they pay, you know, using an econometric model, they, they estimated, you know, where are the critical variables that determine NPLs. And they break down the, the, the portfolio in consumer loans, in mortgage loans, and in corporates. So, once they determine, you know, these econometric model, then they provided a, you know, a shock with the variables that we're seeing, uh, lately, and they estimated how much, uh, NPL ratios, rate for the entire banking system. Um, for consumer loans, that would imply a 32% increase for mortgages 19% and for corporates, also 32%. So, when you do that math and you break down, you know, by types of loans, by type of bank, you get to see, you know, this is obviously a ballpark estimate because obviously every single bank has different practices in origination practices and recoveries and so on, but you can get a sense of the magnitude in terms of using this model, you know, how much you, you will expect at each bank with, with provision, okay? Thank you. Thank, Hector, for the, uh, detailed responses. We will take the next question. It comes from the line of Ernesto <inaudible> from Bank of America. Please go ahead. Hi. Good morning, Hector. Thanks for the opportunity. Uh, my first question is a follow up on the preventive provisions of 349 pesos. So, you think this level of provisions will be enough for the rest of the year? Or are you going to evaluate to create more depending on the behavior of payments up to the relief program. And then my second question is, uh, a different one. Because we would like to know if you have exposure to either Mexico on the chapter 11 or <inaudible> that has restructured loans with the banks for about a year. But I understand that you can not disclose your exposure so maybe if you can remind us how much of your loan book is exposed to airlines and how much to food and beverage and if there is a sector that is concerning you. And finally, uh, my, my last question is, um, competition in mortgage loans, uh, we have seen different banks reducing the interest rates, uh, of mortgages in light of the lower rates, so how has been your strategy to attract more, more mortgage clients and how, uh, competitive is your interest rate? Thank you. Thank you, Ernesto. Uh, let me, let me start and then maybe I will complement. First of all on the <inaudible> of provision, like, um, I have spoken many times to my CRO and he basically tells me that, uh, when I ask you and ask him the same question that you're asking me at this point, and he says, look, I don't have my crystal ball. Um, so clear and it is quite impossible, not impossible, but it is difficult to assess what we're doing, I mean, what I can tell you is that we're doing our best job in, in basically reviewing the portfolio. We do like that we see the rate that we review the, the behavior and we're basically trying to be as conservative as we can in order to manage it, so, um, and if we see that the portfolio continues to deteriorate it's much more complicated. We're going to find out, I mean, the payment shock comes in the first week of August. We're preparing for next week actually, when the first loans that went into the holiday start, um, to, to mature and we, we see that, we see how the performance of the portfolio is and then we'll take, you're going to see our numbers, uh, right as, uh, I mean, we're going to take the provisions as soon as they come if, uh, needed, okay? And the second point, in Mexico, yes, and, and <inaudible>, we do have, uh, exposure to them. Nothing that we cannot control or nothing too big for the size of the bank. I mean, our top exposure to airlines, um, is not big. I mean, compared to the top of the heights of the portfolio, I would say, eh, probably <inaudible> it's not big. It's not very significant versus the size of the bank. And, uh, we have loans or provisions already on some <inaudible> to Mexico so we can tell you that in, in the provisions that we make, some of them are made to, for the exposure which is not relevant. In terms of <inaudible>, yes, we do have exposure to them. Also not, I mean, not significant and we are basically in those structure and process as of now and, uh, we see that the company can recover, uh, fairly, I mean, I don't see that <inaudible> in that particular situation <inaudible>. Unfortunately, in front of me and we can back to you on that one, <inaudible> In terms of the competition of mortgage loans, um, it's quite important what we do, uh, uh, we cannot, important differentiation in terms of how we do mortgages versus our competition. If you take a look at what <inaudible> fees, it's a completely different prototype nobody has replicated in the Mexican market. I mean, some of the other banks basically have tried to do <inaudible> what we are doing <inaudible>. The best rate available in the market. But in order to get the rate, uh, rate available in the market, which is seven points, 75% for a 15 year mortgage, you have to basically turn into, turn first into your most important bank, to your number bank, number one bank. But in order to be able to get that rate, you have to have different products with us that basically you have to have your payroll, you have to direct maybe, you, you <inaudible>, you have to have your insurance, you have to, Something that you have to do in order to access the rate. In that regard, what happens is even if the loan is at 7.75 and we're also very cautious in the loan value that we're doing that, that loan, that's why precisely I was explaining in the presentation that that's why our <inaudible> for mortgage is 37% larger than the rest. So we go directly to the mort- to the wealthy people, okay? To the people that are in the best situation right now. So we believe that our portfolio is more resilient in this particular situations. And also that, um, we get the best client because those are the ones that basically can achieve this rate and can achieve these, and have these characteristics to act in this type of role. So that's why the, the, the portfolio we believe is gonna behave well and is going to be managed, um, or is going be, um, behave in a nice, in a nice way due to that fact, okay? And we have seen the way the portfolio has performed and we see that the performance is according to our expectations given the very complicated circumstances. Okay? So in that regard, I mean, there is competition, I mean, there are some banks, I mean, a couple of banks have tried to come in and, and, basically compete with us head to head in this type of situations and they have not been able to have the same type of problems that we send to the market. Then we came out with another prototype, <inaudible>. The <inaudible> basically has a particular and has everything included in the rate. You don't have any fees, any commissions. You don't have to pay for any ancillary costs or anything like that. So that particular thing is for people that, I mean, that they believe that we have a lot of things under the table and that we're not completely clear, it's completely transparent to the client. And that is above 9% as a rate and, uh, and that's for a difficult, different type of client. So we have a Chinese menu for every different type of <inaudible>, uh, what we do and, and, for example, <inaudible> for a different sector that is mutual, that is basically clients with lower ticket. And, um, well, there are some of these clients are very good and, uh, have very good credit quality and, uh, that's what we decided to compete in that sector. I don't know if these answer your question or not. Please be a complement. Sure, Hector, um, eh, regarding your first, first question, Ernesto, uh, it was, as previously mentioned, uniting <inaudible> on the, you know, how severe is the contraction this year. You know, and how that impacts the capacity for clients to, to pay back their loans. We think that, eventually if there's, uh, you know a U type recovery and GDP contracting somewhere around 10-12 %, then we have made with the provisions of this quarter, more than half of what we require and that could be, you know, 60-75 % of what we require, you know, with information that we have right now, okay? But it's going to be dependent on how this evolves and obviously we're closely monitoring, eh, you know, how our clients are, are behaving, okay. Now on your second question regarding the exposure that you have to Mexican <inaudible>, eh, your obviously for reasons we cannot disclose those, but we can, eh, reassure you that these two exposures that we have are not within our top 10 exposures, eh. To mention, we have already provisions partially exposure with Mexico that's close to 45% of the exposure that we have that is not material, okay. And I don't have with me the breakdown in terms of food and beverage, the breakdown that I have right now it's more, uh, the one that is used more for regulatory purposes and do not have that detail. But, eh, for sure, we can follow up with that. This is super helpful. Thank you very much. We'll take the next question. It comes from Brian <unk> from Citibank. Please go ahead. Uh, everyone thank you for the opportunity to ask question. Before my question, I wanted to make a <inaudible> that <inaudible> capability do so <inaudible>, what was the percentage, sorry. <crosstalk> I think that's 40%, right. 40%, thank you. And, and, now for my question, uh, you know <inaudible> quality and you mentioned <inaudible> portfolio has a large degree of <inaudible>, uh, my question is, could you <inaudible> and the <inaudible>. What are you expecting in terms of NPL for <inaudible>. Thank you. Um, if you like, I can take that, Hector, um, the, you know, credit cards do not have collateral. You know, so, so laws given defaults in, in credit cards is, eh, close to 100%, you know, you, you basically do not, eh, eh recover that much in terms of, eh, eh, credit cards, you know and, you know, we think that that's, that