rs are set up for and we've kind of prepared them for that, they've made the commitment to deliver that, so you know, we just wanna make sure again our sold and unstarted backlog doesn't get too far out for us especially in an environment of rising prices. We know that future land prices, land's gonna cost more in the future and so really its the capacity of our trade partners, so we continue to measure that. Uh, we've gone a little bit lighter on sales releases for that reason and, uh, we think that's the best course of action. And Leonard, if I could add, I really just think it's varies by market and also by newer housing program or older housing program, so, it isn't just a, a one size fits all. Leonard and John have worked pretty carefully at trying to analyze where we have opportunities, uh, and where, um, and where there's more challenges and in some cases, you know, our goal is to build out of older projects and get through it. But, in other cases, if we're not gonna be able to start a house for two or three months, uh, it doesn't make any sense that we put it up for sale in a market that continues to be strong, is… And you know I actually think we're being very logical and we're attempting, uh, to maximize the profits and if you look at the communities we've opened in the last 12 months, you'd see a significant increase in both absorption and margin, uh, and, and this, we're hoping that we're gonna be able to keep that, the absorption steady and, uh, the margins improving, there's non reason why we shouldn't be able to. Great man, uh, I appreciate that extra collar there Larry. And if I could just sneak in one more here, um, you kind of brought up the land market a little bit, um, you know, what are you seeing right now, because you know, now the debt refinancing is behind you guys, you obviously have a little bit more runway and disability from the balance sheet perspective, so I would imagine, you know you're kind of stepping on the gas a little bit here on the land side of things and at the same time I would imagine, you know, your competitors are as well, given how strong the selling environment has been. So, what, what's the viability of, you know, some of those quick return deals that you could kinda put your pen and paper to and potentially get on the ground here over the next 12 or 18 months? Well, it's, it is- Okay, Larry. Let me just start Leonard and then you jump through. Um, you know, land in the markets we're in has always been challenging, always, and one of the strengths of our organization has been that we have land relationships that go back over 30 years, uh, whether with master developers or with individuals, um, and it is tougher now, but we're, we are continuing to find opportunities and, uh, uh, there is no reason why, um, we won't be able to, you know, grow the business over the next two to three years. But, uh, one thing you have to be aware of, and our investors I hope they all understand, is that when California is your strongest market place, uh, it takes a couple years, to, from when you buy a piece of ground, to when you're closing houses. And we've been planning that and we understand that that's the situation we're in, but, uh, don't lose track at, if anything, when the market is particularly tough, or difficult because of, uh, a lot of competition, relationships matter more than ever and that's one thing that we, have and we always had and we always will have. Um, and with that Leonard please, um, keep going. Yeah I think you hit most of the points Larry, I would say Alan we're certainly back in the market and we are kinda book handing this. We're looking for opportunities that we can basically get control of, that we can open our model homes in 12-18 months and because of somewhat limited capital we're looking for opportunities to fill our 23 / 24 pipeline and those, to profile those deals would look quite differently. The land, come market is completely, is really competitive right now as you would expect. We've had some success tying up some interesting opportunities and I would say Arizona is the most competitive land environment today and so that's probably our biggest challenge, is to identify asses that makes sense, there's positions available but we want to make sure they make sense and that we can deliver on those returns. I'd add one other thing to your prior question real quick, um, when you kind of talked about sales pace and running it a little bit harder, I would say even with the steps that we've taken and, you know, we just closed the month of, uh, October. October was a really strong month even with those measures, it's the third strongest sales month in the company's history, where your sales were up 65%, um, and we averaged, average absorption of 3.5 and that's even counting closed out communities where we have, 2 / 3 / 4 homes left to sell that kind of weighs that down a little bit. So, again it's still an extremely strong market and we're kind of really excited about future and what it can mean for the company. Great, thanks a lot guys and that sounds promising and good luck. Thanks. Thanks Alan Thank you. Thank you. Ladies and gentlemen as a reminder if you'd like to join the question queue please press star one on your telephone keypad. Our next question comes from Alex <inaudible> with Housing Research Center. Please proceed with your question. Morning guys, great job. Thanks Alex. Thanks Alex. Thanks Alex. Um, I was curious, um, you've mentioned here starting to do some fee work for five point, do you have any sense of timing you can share with us when those first, uh, deliveries of revenues, well I guess it's more, uh, you book earning, right when construction happens, so can you kind of help, give us an idea on how to model that? Sure, this is, good morning Alex, this is Leonard, yeah, so, I'm sure you're aware it's a slightly different fee structure than say what we've done with the Irvine company in the past, and so in