working full speed regardless of, um, you know, who, who's, you know, president or what the election results are. But certainly it would be good to have, um, you know, the results of the election. I think we've all as a, as a country, um, you know, that, uh, the election results are something that we've all targeted out there, and wherever you fall on the, uh, the political spectrum, it's good to have, uh, certainty as opposed to uncertainties. So I think we're all looking forward to what the final outcome is there so we can, we can move forward. Okay. But just so, it, just so for my clarity, the process of the BOEM is pretty much the, the agency that, that so the bureaucratic process is going on, really you don't see a significant change one way or the other, it, it, regardless of the, the outcome of the presidential election. Is, is that the, am, am I right understanding that? Yeah. I'd say, uh, that the work at the agencies is, is going on. You know, they're, you know, we've been meeting, uh, regularly going through, uh, questions. Uh, we're working through, uh, the various state agencies. So no, I, I'd say that the work is continuing, um, at the, at the, as you say, the, the bureaucrat level. Okay, awesome. Thanks so much. You're welcome. Thanks, Paul. Uh, next question is from Mike Weinstein from <inaudible>. Good morning, Mike. Hey, good morning. Morning, Phil. <crosstalk> Hi, good, uh, hope you're, uh, hope you're doing well. Hey, I, I, maybe you could just give a quick two second update on, uh, what you think, uh, the outcome at FERC might be for, uh, transmission ROEs and, you know, considering, uh, if the election outcome may, has any effect on any of this, uh, in accelerating an outcome. Um, well, Mike, that's a, that's a very, uh, big crystal ball that you're asking <crosstalk>. So, but again, thanks, and I hope you're, you're doing well. Thanks for your question. I, you know, it's, um, I wish I had a, uh, you know, a better answer than to say that, um, you know, it's working its way through. We, we don't really have, um, a specific clarity as to, um, when FERC might, um, you know, come up with something on the New England <inaudible> pending, New England cases, and certainly, um, impact of the election one way or the other, what, what that could have in terms of commissioners and that type of thing. So, um, you know, the only thing I know for certain is we're, we're looking at, uh, our ten point five seven rates, and uh, reserving to that level, and actually 11.74 cap, um, and you know, we'll just have to wait and see what the final, the final outcome will, will look at. But I don't really have a, uh, an answer. I know in, in years past when I've tried to, when I've tried to think that one was coming or it was going a certain way, it really hadn't, hasn't materialized, so I think it's best to wait 'til the, the final, the final order comes out at this point. Or orders, as it may be. Right. Hey, and uh, bigger crystal ball question, uh, would be, uh, you know, I know that Hydra Quebec has a pretty big long term construction plan for gen, you know, hydro generation up there, and I know that their long term plans included lots and lots of Northern Pass type transmission lots. Uh, you know, do you think there's ever a time at some point where there might be another, uh, another wack or another go at transmission at some point? You know, big transmission project? Think a lot of that is dependent upon what the states want to get, right? So this, this, these are gonna be processes now that are driven by state's clean energy policies and the state's desire to, um, um, have, uh, either offshore wind or solar or hydro in, in the mix. So, um, you know, there are certainly, there's a lot of activity at the states now. I mean the states in our area all want, uh, aggressive, uh uh, carbon reduction targets. So it wouldn't be out of the question to see a state, you know, want to, um, you know, contract for, for more of that, but