r ago. Um, we're building out, uh, uh, space and we're putting in more equipment. Um, and the… some of the devices that we're producing are particularly complicated and high-end, which is great for us because it's, uh, uh, it's, uh, more differentiated. Um, but they take more, uh, machine capacity because there's multiple processes, multiple passes, um, versus some of the more straightforward products. And that, that, of… those, of course, the more complicated products often go to the, the more, uh, sensitive and the high-end customers. So, we always wanna favor those. And so we did, uh, uh, have some that we couldn't ship we're catching up in this quarter, um, and it will have the capacity built out, uh, by the end of this quarter. Again, when you talk about building capacity, you're talking, you know, 100, 150 grand in, uh, in facilities, um, you know, build out and, uh, and, uh, few hundred thousand dollars in equipment. It's not a major, uh, capital absorption. Um, but we have been catching up through the third quarter and, uh, we'll be all caught up in a good shape, uh, going into 2021. Okay. And then just the last one, and I'll jump back into queue. I mean, obviously- mm-hmm <affirmative>. the school and education market could be a little challenging- mm-hmm <affirmative>. with budgetary pressures and whatnot, but just curious if you could comment on what your expectations in that market are going forward. If it is sharpy, as you say, but it's also, um, uh, you know, highly motivated for security, uh, we, you know, we, we put out a white paper on, uh, um, remote school in cyber security, for example, with our Fido keys that we just launched. You know, one of the dirty secrets that a lot of people haven't, you know, focused on is, we've taken all the kids in our country and, and, and put them online to, to go to school, and if anyone thinks that we've actually properly secured their, their data pipes, uh, for their learning, um, they're, they're, they're being 